Below is a complete solution for the deepCopy() method written in Java 21. 
The implementation relies on reflection and handles arbitrary object graphs, including collections, arrays, maps, cyclic references (using an IdentityHashMap as a memoization cache), final fields, primitives, enums, proxies, records, and immutable wrappers.
Algorithm Overview
1.	Null & Immutable Handling: Immediately return if input is null or if it is an instance of known immutable types (String, wrapper classes like Integer, Enum).
2.	Cyclic Reference Detection: Before processing any non-primitive object, check if it already exists in the identity-based cache (IdentityHashMap). If found, return the cached copy to prevent infinite recursion.
3.	Array Cloning: Use java.lang.reflect.Array to create new arrays of the same component type and recursively deep-copy each element.
4.	Collection Copying: Instantiate a new collection using its default constructor (falling back to specific implementations like ArrayList if needed) and populate it with copied elements.
5.	Map Copying: Similar to collections, instantiate a new map and put deeply copied key-value pairs into it.
6.	Record Support: For Java records, use the canonical constructor obtained via reflection to build a new instance from copied components.
7.	General Object Copying:
o	Traverse the class hierarchy up to Object.class.
o	Retrieve all declared fields, making them accessible.
o	Skip static fields.
o	Handle final fields by temporarily removing their modifiers via Unsafe (a necessary evil for true generic cloning without serialization).
o	Recursively call deepCopy on field values.
8.	Safety Measures: The code avoids calling user-defined methods (like getters/setters or custom constructors for regular POJOs) to minimize side effects. It also skips synthetic bridge methods when dealing with records.
