// A simple Node class to test self-referential (cyclic) data structures
class Node {
    private String id;
    private List<Node> children;
    private Node parent; // Back-reference creates a cycle

    public Node(String id) {
        this.id = id;
        this.children = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void addChild(Node child) {
        children.add(child);
        child.parent = this;
    }

    public String getId() { return id; }
    public List<Node> getChildren() { return children; }
    public Node getParent() { return parent; }
}

// A container holding various nested types
class ComplexContainer {
    private Man owner;
    private byte[] binaryData;
    private Map<String, List<Man>> registry;

    public ComplexContainer(Man owner, byte[] binaryData, Map<String, List<Man>> registry) {
        this.owner = owner;
        this.binaryData = binaryData;
        this.registry = registry;
    }

    public Man getOwner() { return owner; }
    public byte[] getBinaryData() { return binaryData; }
    public Map<String, List<Man>> getRegistry() { return registry; }
}
Main Demonstration
This main method verifies correctness through three checks:
1.	Structural Equality vs. Instance Independence: Modifying the original does not affect the copy.
2.	Cyclic References: Ensures copying doesn't cause a StackOverflowError and preserves graph topology.
3.	Collections and Primitives: Verifies that inner mutable lists and array contents are duplicated.
